INT. FIELD HOSPITAL - REQUISITIONED BALLROOM - NIGHT

A grand hall of cracked chandeliers and bloodied parquet. Cots in rows where the dance floor used to be. Lanterns hang from candelabras. Somewhere, a gramophone with no needle collects dust.

SERGEANT NIKODEM ZAWADZKI, 30s, left arm in a sling, sits on a cot. Beside him, NURSE PHILOMENA OKONKWO, 30s, rolls bandages with grim efficiency. Across the aisle, a young soldier lies still, face to the wall: PRIVATE ELIAS BRODEUR, 18.

A figure in the doorway, silhouetted by the dying fire outside: a STRANGER, in a civilian overcoat, rifle slung.

STRANGER
(from the door)
Is this the hospital?

PHILOMENA
It's a ballroom. Come in.

The Stranger crosses the hall. His boots echo on the parquet. He pauses by Elias's cot.

STRANGER
This one's alive?

NIKODEM
He breathes. That's all we know.

PHILOMENA
Who are you?

STRANGER
Captain Lucien Marchetti. 4th Brigade. Our line broke at midnight.

NIKODEM
(sitting up)
Broke?

MARCHETTI
They'll be here by dawn.

Philomena rises. Nikodem swings his legs to the floor. Elias stirs, a small groan.

PHILOMENA
We have forty wounded.

MARCHETTI
Then you have a decision.

NIKODEM
We have no decision. We don't move them.

MARCHETTI
He will. And she will. And you will.

He points to Philomena. Then to Nikodem. Then to the wall.

MARCHETTI (CONT'D)
Sergeant, you speak for the men?

NIKODEM
I speak for no one.

MARCHETTI
Then I'll speak.

Elias wakes. Looks at Marchetti. Looks at the rifle.

ELIAS
Captain?

MARCHETTI
Private.

ELIAS
I can walk.

PHILOMENA
No, you can't.

ELIAS
I can crawl.

MARCHETTI
(quietly)
Then crawl first. We follow.

He turns to the others. Nikodem nods once. Philomena grabs a blanket.

They begin moving cots toward the door, one by one, the way mourners carry a coffin.

At the far end of the hall, the gramophone, caught in a draft, begins to turn on its own. A thin old waltz crackles out, ghostly, ridiculous, and every head lifts.

Then the first shell lands.

The chandelier swings. Plaster rains. The cots stagger. Philomena throws herself over Elias. Nikodem hauls Marchetti down. The waltz continues, warped by the blast, slowing to a drone.

Dust. Smoke. A long, long silence.

Marchetti coughs. Rises. Lifts his rifle.

MARCHETTI (CONT'D)
They're early.

NIKODEM
(wiping his face)
Of course they are.

Marchetti smiles grimly and puts his rifle to his shoulder. The ballroom, for the last time, becomes a ballroom of living men.
